protesters against military rule in  sudan facing tear gas and live  ammunition by security forces those  injured are rushed on motorbikes to  hospital where medics most of whom are  volunteers try to provide assistance  i was in front of the protest tear gas  was fired at protesters and i got hit by  a canister it hit me right behind my  left ear  there were shots fired on one of the  streets when we were retreating from the  security forces i got grazed by a bullet  in my head and was rushed to the  hospital  since october last year protesters have  been taken to the streets to voice their  anger against a military takeover which  resulted in the dissolution of a  two-year transitional government meant  to lead sudan to democracy security  services have repeatedly used tear gas  and live ammunition against unarmed  civilians the u.n and other  international bodies have condemned the  use of force which it describes as  excessive  its human rights office says tear gas  canisters fired directly at protesters  is a violation of international  standards  at least seven protesters were killed in  the previous protests to the  presidential palace but that has not  deterred protesters who continue to take  to the street at least 72 people have  been killed in such protests since they  take over three months ago more than 2  000 have been injured  but getting treatment is not easy  ambulances are repeatedly blocked from  reaching those injured hospitals have  been raided by security forces with tear  gas fired inside them and protesters  have even been arrested while receiving  treatment inside hospitals  those who are already sick and bleeding  when the attacks happen and the tear gas  is fired it terrorizes the staff the  medics come to provide assistance and  the attacks make it hard to continue  working until the situation passes  that could be half an hour or an hour  and sometimes we're forced to transfer  patients because of the lack of security  sudan's military-led governing body the  sovereignty council has promised to  protect hospitals and medical facilities  during protests but those on the streets  want the council gone and want  accountability for the lives lost and  those injured demanding a civilian rule  heber morgan al-jazeera cartoon
 

